Dockerizing and Deploying a Python App on App Server 3
This guide provides an overview of the implementation steps, Docker configuration, and testing procedures used to complete the Python application deployment task on App Server 3 (stapp03).

Task Overview
The objective was to containerize an existing Python/Flask application residing on App Server 3, tag the image, launch it as a container with port mapping, and verify its response locally.

Key Requirements
Dockerfile Location: Created under /python_app/Dockerfile.

Base Image: Python (using python:3.9-slim).

Dependencies: Installed via /python_app/src/requirements.txt.

Port Configuration: Exposed port 8089 and mapped it to host port 8099.

Image Name: nautilus/python-app

Container Name: pythonapp_nautilus

Entrypoint Script: Executed server.py using CMD.

Implementation Details
1. Dockerfile Configuration
The Dockerfile was set up inside /python_app to bundle the source files, install dependencies, and run the server with the appropriate host bindings (0.0.0.0) to allow external proxy/port mapping access:

Dockerfile
FROM python:3.9-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY src/requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY src/ .

EXPOSE 8089

CMD ["python", "server.py", "--host=0.0.0.0", "--port=8089"]
2. Build and Deployment Commands
Images were built and containers were run on App Server 3 (banner@stapp03) using the following sequence:

Build the Image:

Bash
sudo docker build -t nautilus/python-app .
Run the Container:

Bash
sudo docker run -d --name pythonapp_nautilus -p 8099:8089 nautilus/python-app
Verification and Testing
To verify that the application container is responding successfully through the mapped port, run the curl test against the host port:

Bash
curl http://localhost:8099/
